# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Echo Waits for the Mallet to Rise

**Story & Context:** At 82 BPM, Thiri opens with a dry four-note pattala cell: low fifth, upper second, tonic, sixth. No delay is present during the first two cycles. Leon enters only after the final note with a short root-to-sixth Bass Afterimage, while May holds a sparse minor-7 Wurlitzer voicing. On the third cycle, the first Rebound Send appears: the upper second receives one tape repeat timed just after Thiri’s soft mallet visibly clears the key. Ko Min keeps the groove to round kick, cross-stick, and relaxed hat. Aaron plays only one upper-string skank on beat three. In the chorus, Return Chord becomes explicit—the pattala states an open interval, the first tape repeat sounds alone, then May reveals a major-6 harmony after the echo.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close-up of a pattala mallet rising above a bamboo key while one old tape-echo reel turns in the background, monsoon rain streaking a glass permit window, warm amber and teak reflections. Original sparse lyric fragments: “wait till the mallet rises / don’t answer yet / rain on the permit glass / I still know your room.”

## Track 2: Frequency Card Folded Under the Teacup

**Story & Context:** At 88 BPM, this is the EP’s firmest lovers-rock groove. Leon plays a deep two-bar bass line using tonic, fifth, flat seventh, and chromatic approach tones. Ko Min adds dry rimshot and a small tambourine only in the refrain. Thiri alternates hard and soft pattala strikes so Rebound Send timing changes within one repeated phrase: hard hits create short single repeats; soft hits leave a wider gap before the echo. Three Bamboo Ducks remove Wurlitzer, bass, and guitar separately after selected strikes, making the wooden attacks feel larger without increasing their level. May sings a close two-part chorus about finding an old frequency permit used as a coaster beneath a teacup years after a relationship ended.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Worn cream radio-frequency card folded beneath a chipped teacup on a teak counter, one circular tea stain crossing an old approval stamp, pattala mallets and a bass string packet nearby, rain-dark green light outside. Original sparse lyric fragments: “frequency card / under the cup / your name under the stain / still licensed in my head.”

## Track 3: River Receiver After Midnight

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 74 BPM. May begins alone on Wurlitzer with two-note sus2 and add9 shapes, leaving the third unstated. Thiri enters with widely spaced pattala notes, each completely dry. Leon waits almost a full bar before his first bass note. When the drums arrive, Ko Min uses kick, cross-stick, and no cymbal for sixteen bars. Bass flute enters as the principal Echo Window source: one breath-heavy fifth is sent into tape delay after a pattala phrase, then the send closes immediately. Midway through, Return Chord turns the same pattala fifth first into minor 7 and later into major 6 without changing the acoustic notes. In the final minute, guitar, drums, Wurlitzer, and voice disappear. Thiri plays one soft bamboo tonic. Leon answers with one low bass note after the rebound, but the tape echo is left closed. The EP ends with natural pattala decay, river rain, and the faint mechanical stop of the tape reel.

